Declarations are private to their module. An `export:` block in `@mod.moth` or `@package.moth` makes them public.

### An internal helper file

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/utils.moth
    internal_value #= 42
]

### The child root uses it directly

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- components/@mod.moth
    @utils internal_value

    export:
        @utils internal_value
    ;
]

Same-module dependencies see helper declarations. No export block is needed inside the helper.

### Root declarations stay in their root

A declaration written directly in `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` or `@entry.moth` belongs to that file only. Helper files cannot see it. Another root file cannot see it. Name the module folder in a dependency instead of the root file.

### Other modules need the export block

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    -- @entry.moth
    @components internal_value
]

Now the parent module can bind `internal_value` through the `@components` public surface. It cannot bypass that surface with `@components/utils`.

An entry-only folder has no public surface to bind. A support-only folder is reachable only through its `@package.moth` surface by eligible consumers.

`export:` remains the current public marker. A later `$export` migration will keep it only in `@mod.moth` and `@package.moth`.
